# AutoLink RadioSense: Neighbour Management Documentation

# Neighbour Management Functions

Design and Implementation Specification

Document purpose: Define the design, behaviour, data structures,
algorithms, integration points, and verification plan for the Neighbour
Management module in the AutoLink RadioSense project.

Status: Design specification for implementation
Target platform: ESP32 using PlatformIO
Communication context: LoRa beacon reception using an SX1278/RA-02-class
radio, subject to confirmation against the project’s current hardware
and driver configuration.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Introduction

AutoLink RadioSense uses beacon packets to announce the state and
location of a node. A receiving node needs to remember which other nodes
it has heard from recently, keep their latest available information,
estimate communication-link quality, and remove entries that are no
longer active.

The Neighbour Management module provides this function through a
Neighbour Table. Each receiving node maintains its own local table. The
table is not automatically shared with other nodes; it represents the
neighbours known to that particular receiver.

This document covers four work-breakdown-structure (WBS) items:

1.  Neighbour Table Design
2.  Neighbour Entry Creation
3.  Neighbour Entry Updation
4.  Neighbour Timeout

It also describes packet-delivery-ratio (PDR) estimation, error
handling, integration with Beacon Management and LoRa reception,
testing, and recommended implementation order.

2. Objectives

The module should:

-   Store a bounded list of recently heard neighbour nodes.
-   Identify each neighbour using its six-byte Node ID (the ESP32 Wi-Fi
    MAC address carried in the beacon).
-   Create an entry when a valid beacon is received from an unknown
    node.
-   Update the existing entry when another valid beacon arrives from a
    known node.
-   Store the latest location, heading, speed, reception time, RSSI, and
    SNR where the radio driver provides those measurements.
-   Estimate PDR using a clearly defined packet-counting method.
-   Expire stale entries after a configurable period without a valid
    beacon.
-   Provide a clean interface for the routing module to inspect the
    current neighbours.
-   Keep the main.cpp file focused on calling module functions rather
    than containing the implementation logic.

3. Scope and assumptions

3.1 In scope

-   In-memory neighbour table on each ESP32.
-   Entry lookup, creation, and update.
-   Reception timestamping using the receiver’s local monotonic clock.
-   RSSI and SNR storage, if available from the radio driver.
-   Basic PDR estimation using beacon sequence numbers and reception
    statistics.
-   Periodic expiry of stale entries.
-   Serial output for development and testing.

3.2 Out of scope for the first version

-   A complete multi-hop routing algorithm.
-   Persistent storage across resets.
-   Cryptographic authentication of beacon senders.
-   A distributed, globally consistent neighbour database.
-   Guaranteed PDR measurement when the sender’s transmission count is
    unknown.
-   Treating RSSI or SNR as proof that a link is reliable.

3.3 Hardware and interface assumptions

This specification assumes that beacon packets are received over LoRa
using an SX1278/RA-02-class radio. Confirm this against the actual board
and current code before implementation. The module should not hard-code
assumptions about a particular LoRa library.

The receive layer should provide: - The raw or decoded beacon packet. -
RSSI for the received packet, if supported by the driver. - SNR for the
received packet, if supported by the driver.

For LoRa, RSSI and SNR are reception measurements. They should normally
be read from the receiver/driver and should not be treated as
sender-supplied beacon fields. Wi-Fi RSSI APIs are not a substitute for
LoRa radio measurements.

The exact packet fields and units must match the project’s existing
BeaconPacket definition. The earlier design uses integer-scaled GPS
coordinates and includes a sequence number. Before implementation,
inspect the current source to confirm every field, unit, packet length,
and sequence-number type.

